          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lafayette-la-princesse-de-cleves-cf641c31155fddef\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"The embedded clause “je vous aimais” (“I loved you”) presents the letter writer’s former loving state as true. It neither undertakes a relational commitment nor functions as a reflex-like affective discharge: it occurs within a deliberate retrospective explanation of how and why she concealed her feelings.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"An embedded avowal in a letter: the writer refers retrospectively to how much she loved its addressee.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The letter writer explains that, after discovering the addressee’s apparent infidelity, she strategically hid her pain and tried to make her passion seem diminished. The highlighted avowal is therefore part of a calculated retrospective disclosure, not one of the deliberately cold earlier communications she describes. Its strategic and jealous context affects interpretation but does not alter its core truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“je ne voulus pas que vous eussiez le plaisir de montrer combien je vous aimais”\",\"supports\":\"The embedded phrase presupposes and avows the extent of the writer’s past love, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The writer says she resolved to hide her pain and make her passion appear to be weakening.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance belongs to reflective, deliberate narration rather than expressive or reflex-like discharge, supporting E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The passage is explicitly framed as a “LETTRE” addressed to the allegedly unfaithful lover.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes written status and the retrospective interpersonal setting.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The writer describes composing “lettres tièdes et languissantes” as part of a plan to conceal her continuing feelings.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding strategy concerns concealment and revenge; it supplies no independent evidence that the highlighted clause enacts a commitment or constitutes affective discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T alone naturally captures the core force as a retrospective written avowal of a loving state; the strategic surrounding context requires no additional core category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The highlighted occurrence is the first “je vous aimais” in the supplied letter, embedded in “combien je vous aimais,” rather than the later phrase about forcing herself to say and write that she loved him.\"}"
        }
